Lab 10: Red Hat Ceph Storage Integration with OpenStack
Lab Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Install and configure Red Hat Ceph Storage (RHCS).
Integrate Ceph RADOS Block Device (RBD) with OpenStack Cinder for block storage.
Configure and manage storage policies in OpenStack.
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, ensure you have:

A running Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL) 8/9 system with root/sudo access.
A deployed OpenStack environment (Minimal: Controller + Compute + Cinder).
Network connectivity between OpenStack nodes and Ceph cluster.
Basic familiarity with Ceph, OpenStack, and Linux CLI.
Lab Tasks
Task 1: Install and Configure Red Hat Ceph Storage
Subtask 1.1: Set Up Ceph Cluster
Install Ceph packages on the Ceph nodes (monitor, OSD, and manager):

sudo dnf install -y ceph-common ceph-mon ceph-osd ceph-mgr
Initialize the Ceph cluster (run on the first monitor node):

sudo ceph-deploy new <monitor-node-hostname>
Install Ceph on all nodes:

sudo ceph-deploy install <node1> <node2> <node3>
Deploy initial monitors and gather keys:

sudo ceph-deploy mon create-initial
sudo ceph-deploy admin <admin-node>
Expected Outcome:

A basic Ceph cluster is deployed with monitor and OSDs.
Verify with:
sudo ceph -s
Subtask 1.2: Create a Ceph Pool for OpenStack
Create a replicated pool for Cinder volumes:

sudo ceph osd pool create volumes 128
sudo ceph osd pool create vms 128
Enable RBD on the pools:

sudo rbd pool init volumes
sudo rbd pool init vms
Expected Outcome:

Two Ceph pools (volumes and vms) are created and ready for OpenStack integration.
Task 2: Integrate Ceph RBD with OpenStack Cinder
Subtask 2.1: Configure Ceph Client Authentication
Create a Ceph client for Cinder:

sudo ceph auth get-or-create client.cinder mon 'allow r' osd 'allow class-read object_prefix rbd_children, allow rwx pool=volumes, allow rwx pool=vms'
Copy the keyring to the OpenStack controller:

sudo scp /etc/ceph/ceph.client.cinder.keyring <openstack-controller>:/etc/ceph/
Subtask 2.2: Configure Cinder to Use Ceph RBD
Edit /etc/cinder/cinder.conf on the OpenStack controller:

[DEFAULT]
enabled_backends = ceph

[ceph]
volume_driver = cinder.volume.drivers.rbd.RBDDriver
rbd_pool = volumes
rbd_ceph_conf = /etc/ceph/ceph.conf
rbd_flatten_volume_from_snapshot = true
rbd_max_clone_depth = 5
rbd_store_chunk_size = 4
rados_connect_timeout = -1
rbd_user = cinder
rbd_secret_uuid = <generated-uuid>
Restart Cinder services:

sudo systemctl restart openstack-cinder-volume
sudo systemctl restart openstack-cinder-api
Expected Outcome:

Cinder is now configured to use Ceph RBD as a backend.
Verify with:
openstack volume service list
Task 3: Configure and Manage Storage Policies
Subtask 3.1: Create a Ceph-Backed Storage Policy
Define a new volume type:

openstack volume type create ceph-rbd
Set extra specs for Ceph backend:

openstack volume type set --property volume_backend_name=ceph ceph-rbd
Subtask 3.2: Test Volume Creation
Create a volume using the Ceph policy:

openstack volume create --size 10 --type ceph-rbd test-ceph-volume
Verify volume status:

openstack volume show test-ceph-volume
Expected Outcome:

A new Ceph-backed volume is successfully created and listed in OpenStack.
Conclusion
In this lab, you:
✔ Installed and configured Red Hat Ceph Storage (RHCS).
✔ Integrated Ceph RBD with OpenStack Cinder for block storage.
✔ Created and managed storage policies for Ceph-backed volumes.

Next Steps:

